What:		/sys/kernel/security/lua/shdict
Date:		November 2026
Contact:	Lua-LSM project (https://github.com/openanolis/lua-lsm-kernel/issues)
Description:
		This testing interface lets an administrator inspect or update
		a named dictionary in a loaded Lua-LSM policy. It is provided
		when Lua-LSM and securityfs are available. The file mode is
		0600. Opening, reading and writing require CAP_MAC_ADMIN in
		the initial user namespace, in addition to file permissions.

		Each write supplies one request, at most 4096 bytes including
		any line ending. An optional trailing CR/LF is stripped.
		Literal NUL, CR or LF within the request is not accepted.
		Spaces and tabs separate tokens. Module, dictionary and key
		names must each be 1 to 128 bytes, without ASCII whitespace,
		control bytes or DEL. The accepted forms are:

		  get MODULE DICT KEY
		  set MODULE DICT KEY boolean true|false
		  set MODULE DICT KEY number VALUE
		  set MODULE DICT KEY string VALUE
		  set MODULE DICT KEY hex VALUE
		  delete MODULE DICT KEY
		  incr MODULE DICT KEY [DELTA]

		Numeric operands are signed 64-bit decimal integers. The
		default increment is 1; incrementing a missing key sets it
		to the delta. An out-of-range increment returns ERANGE without
		modifying the value. String and hex take the rest of the input
		after separators; hex encodes each byte as two digits and
		allows binary string data. An empty string is valid.

		A read on the same open file description returns the result of
		the most recent successful request: true for set
		or delete, the decimal result of incr, or the scalar value of
		get. String results are raw bytes; an empty string returns
		zero bytes. A missing key on get returns ENOENT at write time.
		The response has no added newline and is at most 4096 bytes.
		A write which enters request processing clears any previous
		response before parsing, including if that parsing fails.

		Reading a freshly opened file without a request enumerates
		keys in LIVE modules. Each line contains the module, dictionary,
		key, type and value separated by spaces. Non-printable bytes
		and backslashes are escaped as \xNN. Lightuserdata is shown
		as <hidden>, without disclosing an address. Empty dictionaries
		produce no rows.

		A successful enumeration is a point-in-time view of the
		exported data. Sustained updates may cause EAGAIN; retry with
		backoff. Output up to and including 65536 bytes is accepted;
		oversized output fails with E2BIG rather than being truncated.
		Once prepared, the result remains unchanged across partial
		reads on that open unless replaced by a write.

		This is a stream interface: seek and positioned I/O are not
		supported. Descriptors shared by dup() or fork() also share the
		buffer and read cursor; they do not provide a write/read
		transaction. Use separate opens for independent operations.
		The interface is experimental and is not a stable ABI.
